 know so focused  on like in hong kong or or you know  afghanistan we had special taylor  tailored schemes for dealing with the  refugees that were bound to come and  bound to be and we should have done the  same with ukraine but instead we had the  home office kind of putting their  fingers in their ears and saying la la  la la la you know and as people were  coming across the border they were still  resisting it but you will remember no  ukrainians being told you can pick fruit  if you want to come into the uk i mean  really insulting and we should have had  a proper scheme ready to go  and you know everything and although  slowly slowly they're getting into the  right place it's kind of such resistance  you know we had a time only a few weeks  ago when poor ukrainians were trailing  around europe trying to find a british  embassy in order to go into the right  place to fill in the paperwork now at  least it's online but it's still really  cumbersome so we really do need to have  a better system than this what we've  always said is that people should be  able to come here but once they're here  we need to make sure we have a record of  who they are and where they're going  particularly in order to make sure that  we safeguard the children can i ask you  about rishi sooner indeed about about  rishi sunak's partner and and i wonder  if do you have any sympathy for the  situation in which she finds herself in  i mean you are someone who you know has  been involved in criticism about the  wealth of your partner as well at some  point the situations are not parallel  because of course she has non-domiciled  status but i i just wonder whether or  not the kind of the private lives of  politicians partners  you might see that as perhaps being the  line is perhaps likely becoming blurred  in this story so first of all um just so  that there's no kind of misunderstanding  about this my husband's a judge um he uh  everybody knows that um he's been  knighted because he's a judge and  therefore i could call myself lady  newjie if i don't um it's a well-paid  job he's a public servant he pays all  his taxes um  if you are a minister in this country  you need to declare not just your own  financial interest but also that of your  spouse in order to make sure that there  are no conflicts of interest and it  seems to me that there is a clear  conflict of interest here because the  chancellor of the exchequer is  responsible for the rules on non-doms he  has a wife who has decided and she made  a positive decision to become a non-dom  which meant that she doesn't pay tax on  her worldwide income but only on her  income in the uk so is labour currently  advocating for non-dom status to be  removed  now what we're saying is that what she's  what what she's done is perfectly legal  but whether we think that it's fair  given that her husband is expecting so  many of us to be making so many  sacrifices he's increasing taxes 15  taxes he's increased he's expecting us  all to pay our to pull our weight but  not his partner but isn't it but isn't  he true then isn't he accurate then when  he says in the sun says to tahari ko  that the labour party is using his wife  to get to him i mean that that is what's  happening no because he should have  declared that his wife was a non-dom  because he's responsible for the rules  on non-doms so if there had been a  discussion in the treasury about i mean  let's say you look at the this  particular example or other examples of  non-doms and if you look at the details  it does seem a bit odd you know  sometimes you know i mean like she's  been here eight years she's got kids  she's living in downing street and her  taxpayers flat but she's not here as a  permanent resident i mean it does seem  odd you know so it may be that they may  have had discussions in the treasury  about updating these rules making sure  that they're more relevant would he been  involved in those discussions he  shouldn't have been and he 